# Typing Indicator — a chat-shaped proof of `input`'s new on-change dispatch:
#
#   input bind draft placeholder "message" on change -> typingChanged(draft) debounce 1s
#
# Before this, a two-way control could only ever change server state LATER,
# when some button/form got clicked and happened to read the same cell —
# there was no way for the act of typing itself to invoke a server action. A
# typing indicator needs exactly that: the OTHER person's screen has to learn
# "they are typing" the moment typing starts, not after somebody submits.
#
# `after` — the language's other client-only timer — is deliberately NOT used
# to clear the indicator for the other viewer: its body may only assign a
# @client cell (ast.After's own doc: no entity access, no call/do, nothing
# that could reach the authority), so it has no way to make a Typing row
# disappear for anyone but the typer's own browser, and "so-and-so is typing"
# is by definition a fact the OTHER person's client can only learn from the
# server. So the clear side reuses `job … every Ns`, the language's other
# existing timer, the same way examples/inbox.fct already ages rows out on a
# schedule: `tickTyping` counts a live typer down once a second, and whoever's
# `typingChanged` dispatch stops arriving (because they stopped typing) simply
# stops resetting the count, so it runs out and the row is removed. No clock
# comparison anywhere — which matters, because a `remove/set … where`
# predicate must be pure (no `now()`, checked by internal/ir/build.go's
# checkPure), and a ticking counter never needs one.
#
#   facet dev examples/typing_indicator.fct
#
# Try it as two people, two tabs: /?as=ada and /?as=bo. Type in ada's box —
# bo's screen shows "ada is typing…" within ~1s of the keystroke (debounced:
# one dispatch per pause, not one per character), and it clears a few seconds
# after ada stops. Neither tab's input field ever disables or loses focus
# while its own actor is typing through it.

app TypingIndicator:
    entity Message:
        id: int
        author: text
        body: text
        created: int

    # @ephemeral: never touches the durable store (see examples/zones.fct) —
    # exactly right for "is typing," which nobody wants a history of. `read:`
    # hides an actor's own row from themselves (there's no reason to tell
    # someone they are typing) and, composed with the SSE fanout every other
    # `read:`-gated entity already gets, is the entire mechanism by which the
    # OTHER tab sees this live.
    entity Typing @ephemeral:
        id: int
        who: text
        ticksLeft: int
        read: who != actor

    state draft: text = "" @client

    action send(body: text):
        remove t in Typing where t.who == actor
        add Message { author: actor, body: body, created: now() }

    # Fired (debounced) by the input itself as the actor types — the dispatch
    # this file exists to prove. Upserts one row per typer and resets its
    # countdown; someone who keeps typing keeps resetting it before it can
    # ever reach zero.
    action typingChanged(draft: text):
        remove t in Typing where t.who == actor
        add Typing { who: actor, ticksLeft: 3 }

    # The clock-driven half of "clears when they stop": no more keystrokes
    # means no more typingChanged dispatches to reset the count, so three
    # seconds after the last one this reaches zero and the row is removed.
    action tickTyping():
        set t in Typing where true:
            ticksLeft = t.ticksLeft - 1
        remove t in Typing where t.ticksLeft <= 0

    job TickTyping every 1s -> tickTyping

    view Main at "/":
        box:
            text "signed in as {actor}"
            for m in Message by created limit 50:
                text "{m.author}: {m.body}"
            for t in Typing:
                text "{t.who} is typing…"
            input bind draft placeholder "message" on change -> typingChanged(draft) debounce 1s
            button "send" -> send(draft)
